    {
        "id": "engineering-fin-heat-transfer",
        "q": "How does a cooling fin actually work, and when is adding one worth it?",
        "a": "A fin adds surface area to shed heat, but physically it is a tug of war between two effects the thermal core already models: conduction ALONG the fin (the group k*Ac carries heat out to the tip) versus convection OFF its surface (h*P strips that heat to the fluid). Solving the 1-D fin equation collapses the whole balance onto ONE dimensionless number, mL, where m = sqrt(h*P/(k*Ac)). Everything follows from it: temperature decays as cosh(m(L-x))/cosh(mL), heat rate is Q = sqrt(h*P*k*Ac)*thetaB*tanh(mL) = M*tanh(mL), and fin efficiency is eta = tanh(mL)/mL. Because tanh(mL)/mL -> 1 as mL -> 0 and -> 0 as mL -> infinity, a short/fat/high-conductivity fin is nearly isothermal (ideal), while a long fin's tip is dead weight - past about mL ~ 2.3 the tanh has saturated and extra length barely raises Q at all. That is the diminishing-returns law of finning. Effectiveness eps = Q/(h*Ac*thetaB) compares the fin to NO fin (the bare base area); it must clear ~2 to justify the fin, and eps = sqrt(k*P/(h*Ac))*tanh(mL) FALLS as h rises - the key insight: a fin only helps when convection is the bottleneck, so effectiveness is high for a high-k fin in low-h air and drops toward 1 in vigorous (high-h) flow on a poor conductor. This composes the thermal core directly: k comes from MATERIALS and h is the same convection coefficient.",
        "evidence": "The smoke IS the proof (75 assertions, no app): both efficiency limits (eta -> 1 at mL -> 0, eta -> 0 at mL -> infinity) plus monotone decrease with length; Q rising but saturating (the last length-doubling adds less heat than the previous, Q -> M as tanh -> 1); eps > 2 for a copper fin in air and eps < 2 for a stainless fin in boiling flow, with eps strictly decreasing as h rises; a high-k fin beating a low-k fin of identical geometry (copper > aluminum > steel on both eta and Q); and a hand-computed Incropera/Cengel textbook case (k=200 W/mK, h=20 W/m2K, 40x2x40 mm rectangular fin into 20 C air) pinned to m = sqrt(105) = 10.247 /m, Q = 5.0939 W, eta = 0.94752, eps = 39.80, tip temperature 93.72 C.",
    },
    {
        "id": "engineering-riveted-joint-core",
        "q": "How does a riveted or bolted lap/butt joint (the classic boiler seam) get sized, and what is joint efficiency?",
        "a": "A riveted lap or butt joint is rated over ONE repeating PITCH length p, because the seam just repeats. Three failure modes compete, each a force per pitch: the plate can TEAR across its net width, Pt = sigma_t*(p-d)*t; the rivets can SHEAR across their shanks, Ps = tau*(pi/4*d^2)*n*planes; and a rivet can CRUSH (bear on) its plate on the projected area, Pc = sigma_c*d*t*n. The WEAKEST of the three governs - the joint fails there - and the core reports which. Joint EFFICIENCY is the headline number: eta = min(Pt, Ps, Pc) / (sigma_t*p*t), the seam strength divided by the strength of the same but un-drilled solid plate. It is always in (0,1) because the holes can only weaken the plate. A GOOD design BALANCES the three modes: if one mode is far stronger than the others its extra metal is wasted, so peak efficiency for a given rivet arrangement is reached when tearing, shearing and crushing come out nearly equal. A double-cover (double-strap) butt joint puts every rivet in DOUBLE shear (2 planes vs 1 for a lap or single-strap joint), doubling Ps - the main reason to choose it for a boiler seam. This is the joint-design sibling of the bolt/weld connection lane, adding the tearing mode and the efficiency concept that lane never had.",
        "evidence": "The smoke IS the proof (67 assertions, no app). The EFFICIENCY definition is checked as an invariant across four cases: eta is strictly in (0,1) and exactly equals strength/solidPlate, and strength equals the least of the three modes. GOVERNING-MODE FLIP is proven by constructing a case where each mode governs in turn (small net width -> tearing; small rivet + low tau -> shearing; thin plate + big rivet -> crushing) plus a pitch sweep where Ps and Pc stay constant while Pt grows, flipping the weak link tearing -> shearing. BALANCED beats UNBALANCED at the SAME solid plate (60,000 N): tiny rivets starve the shear mode (eta ~ 20.9%) while a well-sized rivet brings the modes together (eta ~ 67.9%). SHEAR PLANES: a double-cover butt joint's Ps is exactly 2x the lap Ps for the same rivets. Adding rivets scales Ps and Pc linearly but leaves Pt = sigma_t*(p-d)*t unchanged (only one hole's width is torn per row), so the plate eventually tears. The TEXTBOOK anchor is Khurmi Example 9.2, a double-riveted lap joint (t=15, d=25, p=75, sigma_t=400, tau=320, sigma_c=640): Pt=300 kN governs (< Ps=314 kN < Pc=480 kN), solid plate=450 kN, eta=300/450=66.7% - matching the published answer.",
    },
